;;Lesson 1

(define (double x) (+ x x))

(query lesson1-check (Marginal (double 3)))

;; Write a function triple that triples its argument using only + (no *), and query (triple 4). Predict the answer before running it.

(define (triple x) (+ x (+ x x)))

(query triple-check (Marginal (triple 4))) ;; Answer should be: 12   1

;;Write a function is-six that takes a number and returns whether it equals 6, using nat=?. Query it on both (double 3) and (triple 3). Predict both answers before running. Double 3 is 6. Is triple 3?

(define (is-six x) (nat=? 6 x))

(query double-three (Marginal (is-six (double 3)))) ;; Answer should be: true   1.0

(query triple-three (Marginal (is-six (triple 3)))) ;; Answer should be: false  1:0 

;;Lesson 2

;;Write a function `lopsided-coin` using (flip 0.9), returning 1 for heads and 0 for tails. Before running it, predict the distribution.

(define (lopsided-coin)
    (if (flip 0.9)
    1
    0)) 

(query lopsided-out (Marginal (lopsided-coin)))
    
;; Predicted 
;; 1   0.9
;; 0   0.1


;; Actual (with floating point rounding on 1-0.9)
;; 1  0.9
;; 0  0.09999999999999998

;;Write a function that flips two independent coins and returns (True) only if both come up heads, using and. Predict the probability of true before running.

(define (two-coin)
    (and (flip 0.5) (flip 0.5)))
   

;; Probability of true: 0.25, expect 1 0.25, 0 0.75

(query two-heads (Marginal (two-coin)))

;;Lesson 3

;;Using only flip, write a query that checks whether two separately-flipped coins disagree

(query two-separate 
    (Marginal (and (flip 0.5) (flip 0.5))))

;; Predict true 0.25, false 0.75


;;and a second query that checks whether one coin, bound with let, disagrees with itself. Predict both answers before running.

(query two-same
    (let ((coin-a (flip 0.5)))
        (Marginal (and coin-a coin-a))))

;;Predict true 0.5, false 0.5

;;Predict, then verify: does (let ((a (flip 0.5)) (b (flip 0.5))) (and a b)) behave like two-independent above, or like one-shared? Why?

;; Like two-independent, because the list in let is binding two separate fair coins. A will always result true 0.5, as will b. So (and a b) probability is 0.25

(query flippy-coins
    (let ((a (flip 0.5)) (b (flip 0.5))) 
    (Marginal (and a b))))


;; Lesson 4

;; Write a function make-adder that takes a number n and returns a function (via lambda) that adds n to its argument. Build add5 from it and query (add5 10).

(define (make-adder n)
    (lambda x -> (+ x n)))

(define add5 (make-adder 5))

(query add5-to-10
    (Marginal (add5 10)))


;; Write a function make-biased-coin that takes a probability p and returns a zero-argument function that flips a coin with that bias. (Recall that (lambda -> body) is sugar for a one-argument function over (Unit).) Build two different coins from it, coin-a at 0.2 and coin-b at 0.8, and query each.

(define (make-biased-coin p)
    (lambda -> (flip p)))

(define coin-a (make-biased-coin 0.2))
(define coin-b (make-biased-coin 0.8))

(query coin-a-out (Marginal (coin-a)))

(query coin-b-out (Marginal (coin-b)))

;; Predict coin-a-out true 0.2 false 0.8, coin-b-out true 0.8, false 0.2

;; Lesson 5

;;Write a query for the sum of two independent (uniform 1 2 3 4 5 6) dice. Before running, predict the shape. Is every total from 2 to 12 equally likely?

(define dice-1 (uniform 1 2 3 4 5 6))
(define dice-2 (uniform 1 2 3 4 5 6))

(query add-die (Marginal (+ dice-1 dice-2)))

;; predict two straight lines meeting at a point: 
;; 2   0.0277777778
;; 3   0.0555555556
;; 4   0.1666666667
;; 5   0.2222222222
;; 6   0.2777777778
;; 7   0.3333333333
;; 8   0.2777777778
;; 9   0.2222222222
;; 10  0.1666666667
;; 11  0.0555555556
;; 12  0.0277777778

;;Using discrete, model a weighted spinner with three outcomes: 1 at 10%, 2 at 60%, 3 at 30%. Query it. Then write a second query for whether the spinner landed on 2, reusing the same function rather than rewriting the discrete expression. (This is Lesson 3’s point in disguise: you don’t need let here, since each query calling the spinner fresh is intentional, but see if you can say why this setup does not risk the double-sampling bug from Lesson 3.)

(define (spinner) (discrete (1 0.1) (2 0.6) (3 0.3)))

(query spinner-test (Marginal (spinner)))
(query spinner-test-return (Marginal (nat=? (spinner) 2)))

;; It's not double-sampling because you're calling the spinner twice for two separate queries. The first one checks spinner's weighted distribution, the second one gives you the probability of spinner returning 2. The bug in lesson 3 was when you meant one coin flip used for a combination but you coded for two independent coin flips. 

;;Lesson 6

;; Define a type mood with three constructors of your choosing, say Calm, Restless, Drowsy. Write a function that generates a random mood with unequal weights, and a second function that maps each mood to a boolean, for instance “is this a mood where meditation would be difficult right now?” Query the resulting distribution.

(define-type mood (Calm)(Restless)(Drowsy))

(define (random-mood)
    (discrete ((Calm) 0.5)((Restless) 0.2)((Drowsy) 0.3)))

(define (good-to-meditate m)
    (match m
      Calm => (True)
      Restless => (True)
      Drowsy => (False)))

(query test-meditate 
    (Marginal(good-to-meditate(random-mood))))

;; Predict test-meditate True 0.7, False 0.3

;; Write a function same-weather? using constructor=? that checks whether two weather values are the same. Query whether two independently-generated random weathers match. Predict the answer by hand first: P(both Sunny) + P(both Rainy) + P(both Cloudy), using the 0.6/0.2/0.2 weights.

(define-type weather (Sunny)(Rainy)(Cloudy))

(define (make-weather)
    (discrete ((Sunny) 0.5) ((Rainy) 0.3) ((Cloudy) 0.2)))

(define (same-weather? weather1 weather2)
    (constructor=? weather1 weather2))

(query same-weather-test
    (Marginal (same-weather? (make-weather)(make-weather))))

;; Predict P(both Sunny) + P(both Rainy) + P(both Cloudy) = (0.5*0.5) + (0.3*0.3) + (0.2*0.2) = 0.38

;; true 0.38, false 0.62

;; Lesson 7

;; checking my understanding

(define my-list (Cons 1 (Cons 2 (Cons 3 (Cons 4 (Nil))))))

(query (Marginal my-list))

(define (my-length l)
  (match l
    Nil => 0
    Cons x rest => (+ 1 (my-length rest))))

(query length-test (Marginal (my-length my-list)))

;; Write a function random-list that builds a list of random coin flips. At each step, flip a fair coin to decide whether to stop, returning (Nil), or continue, consing a fresh (flip 0.5) result onto a recursively-built rest of the list. This list is unbounded in principle: there is no limit on how long it could get. Write a query that asks only whether the list is empty, its first constructor, not its full length. Then, separately, try querying (my-length (random-list)) and see what happens. Lesson 8 explains the result.

(define (random-list)
    (if (flip 0.5)
      (Nil)
      (Cons (flip 0.5) (random-list))))

(define (empty-list l) 
    (match l
      Nil => (True)
      Cons x rest => (False)))

(query random-list-empty? 
    (Marginal (empty-list (random-list))))

(query random-list-length-test 
    (Marginal (my-length (random-list))))

;; StackOverflowError from random-list-length-test query